Independent verdict on the candidate statements. A and B are proved in n5–n6, including each sphere's non-pluripolarity, the actual accumulation-point value, the exact D-normalization, every lower radial product, a uniform global Hessian bound, a genuine C^(0,1/2) potential, and entire finite positive corrected mass. C and D are proved in n7 for every bounded measurable density against ρ, including densities vanishing on arbitrary measurable subsets. D uses a separate exact theorem and has sup=0. E is proved in n8 for each λ>0 with the same geometric reference and same datum, unique in bounded genuine potentials, with no additional sup normalization. F's applications are verified in n1–n4 and n7–n8, using no positive lower mass or BMP assumption and no general global nonclosed mixed-product multilinearity theorem.

The explicit condition M_ρ(ν)<∞ is non-tautological: its definition involves only measures, the prescribed spheres and an essential-supremum test. The fixed-reference version is intrinsic for that fixed measure. The existence-of-some-geometric-configuration version is invariant under transport of holomorphic coordinate data. It does not assert that different reference measures define the same admissible measures. The datum ρ itself passes with M=1 and is singular with respect to every smooth volume; every nonzero ν≤Mρ remains substantively singular. All implications are for actual canonical values.

Zero-lower-mass and infinite-upper-mass qualifications. The proof works for ANY given smooth positive Hermitian h, without inspecting v₋ or v₊. Consequently, IF a given h has v₋(h)=0, it still has this explicit singular datum and family with the three proved implications. The same conditional coverage holds for v₊(h)=∞. Finiteness comes from each actual bounded potential and from the exact finite target equations. We have not exhibited a compact Hermitian manifold with either exceptional global mass behavior. Neither such existence nor a positive infimum is inferred from the construction.

Separate original S15 assessment. As a concrete sufficient criterion, this package meets the mathematical requirements of the allowed sufficient-condition route: it treats truly singular non-pluripolar data, distinguishes all three problems, and bypasses the positive-lower-mass universal theorem. Its substantive prior-work overlap must nevertheless be acknowledged. The general Hölder-subsolution-to-exact-scaled implication is already KN's theorem; the bounded-reference-to-bounded-exponential implication is already ALS's theorem. Our reconstruction supplies an explicit radial/cutoff reference, exact constants and a purely geometric sufficient test within those existing theorem classes. It does not establish literature novelty of the spherical criterion or a new general analytic solvability theorem.

Accordingly, complete original S15 closure or research novelty is NOT certified here. If “new sufficient-condition theorem” accepts a newly reconstructed explicit geometric corollary of the verified primary results, the mathematical sufficient branch is furnished. If it requires a substantive new solvability result beyond those prior theorem classes, that requirement remains unresolved. No necessary criterion, verdict at M=∞, optimal coefficient, unbounded exponential classification, general prescribed singularity result, or P11 K3 bounded-infimum conclusion is claimed. The final candidate flag is false rather than silently promoting this explicit corollary to a complete novel solution of the unchanged original problem.

